{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Summary**\n\n**Early Life and Accession**\nRaymond III (c. 1140–1187) was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at age twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Due to his young age he initially ruled under the regency of his mother Hodierna and his uncle King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond III was raised in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a close ally of the royal family.\n\n**Political and Military Career**\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. In 1164 he was captured at the Battle of Harim and spent nearly ten years imprisoned in Aleppo. After his release in 1174 he returned to Tripoli and resumed his role as count. Raymond III became a key figure in the faction opposing Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. He supported Raymond of Antioch and other nobles against the growing power of the Muslim forces. In 1177 he allied with King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem to defeat Saladin at the Battle of Montgisard. Raymond III also served as regent for Baldwin IV from 1177 to 1185 during the king’s illness. He worked to stabilize the kingdom amid power struggles between factions led by Guy of Lusignan and the Ibelins.\n\n**Death and Legacy**\nRaymond III died in 1187 without a male heir. His death marked the end of the direct line of Raymond of Saint-Gilles who had founded the County of Tripoli. His nephew Raymond IV of Toulouse later inherited the county but it was contested by other claimants. Raymond III is remembered as a capable ruler and military leader who defended the Crusader states during a period of increasing Muslim pressure. His regency helped maintain stability in the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent time.\n\n*highlighted section part 1* **Early Life and Accession**\n\n*